A multi-agent architecture (delegating work across multiple independent agents) introduces substantial distributed systems complexity: network latency, non-deterministic failure modes, distributed tracing overhead, and multiplied token expenses. It is justified ONLY when one or more of the following fundamental architectural boundaries exist:

1. Organizational / Trust Boundaries: Specialist capabilities are operated by different teams, departments, or external third-party vendors who cannot or will not share codebases, private databases, or direct server access;
2. Heterogeneous Technology Stacks: One agent requires a Python machine learning environment with GPU access, while another requires a Java enterprise ledger runtime or specialized C++ simulation tools;
3. Strict Privilege Separation: A high-security financial approval agent operates in an isolated VPC with restricted credentials, delegating low-security data retrieval tasks to an external worker agent without exposing its database credentials;
4. Asynchronous Long-Running Task Specialization: Distinct domains require specialized multi-turn reflection, human-in-the-loop review, or multi-day asynchronous task lifecycles.

If all components run in the same organization, share the same trust boundary, and execute synchronously, a single agent equipped with modular tools or a deterministic StateGraph (LangGraph) is vastly superior in performance, cost, and maintainability.
